+### `git_worktrees`
+
+Accepts a boolean, and defaults to `false`. When enabled, an .envrc file inside a linked git worktree (created with `git worktree add`) is implicitly allowed when both of these are true:
+
+* It is byte-for-byte identical to the .envrc file at the same relative path in the main worktree.
+* That main worktree .envrc file has been allowed with `direnv allow`, or by a `prefix` or `exact` whitelist.
+
+This avoids running `direnv allow` again in every new worktree, while still asking for approval when a branch changes its .envrc. A `direnv deny` on the main worktree .envrc file, or on the worktree .envrc file itself, is respected. Trust is not passed on from one linked worktree to another, and bare repositories have no main worktree, so their linked worktrees are not covered.
+
+Only the .envrc file is compared. Files it loads, such as a `flake.nix` used by `use flake`, can still differ between branches.
+
+Example:
+
+```toml
+[whitelist]
+git_worktrees = true
+```
+
